fr:mainmatter.html:p:
A special case of \Pi -type is the ordinary function type.
Using weakening rule we can obtain thee type A\to  B of ordinary function from A to B

    \frac {
        \Gamma \vdash  A\space \text {type}
        \quad 
        \Gamma \vdash  B\space \text {type}
    }{\dfrac {
        \Gamma ,x:A\vdash  B\space \text {type}
    }{
        \Gamma \vdash  \Pi _{(x:A)}B\space \text {type}
    }\Pi }W

A term f: \Pi _{(x:A)}B is an ordinary function. The type A \to  B is defined:

    A\to  B := \Pi _{(x:A)}B

The type A is called domain of f,
and type B is called codomain of f.
The notation := here means to make a definition.

Consider a type A in context \Gamma . A family of types over A in context \Gamma is a type B(x) in context \Gamma , x:A.
\Gamma , x:A \vdash B(x) \space \text {type}
B is a family of types over A in context \Gamma . Alternatively, we say that B(x) is a type indexed by x:A in context \Gamma .

